<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<RecordType xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THE BROKER CONTACT. Every Contact that existed before 2026-08-10 is this type, and
        every Contact the acquisitions pipeline (Lead conversion, the Broker Portal, manual
        acquisitions entry) creates is this type.

        CONTACT RECORD TYPES DRIVE LAYOUT, FLS AND REPORTING ONLY - NOT SHARING. Contact
        sharing is inherited from its Account via ControlledByParent (target state - see the
        comment in Account/recordTypes/Broker_Firm.recordType-meta.xml for the org's current,
        measured OWD, which has not yet been corrected to match this design). MEASURED, not
        assumed: Contact's live InternalSharingModel was queried via the Tooling API
        (`SELECT InternalSharingModel FROM EntityDefinition WHERE DeveloperName = 'Contact'`)
        on 2026-08-10 (Task 0 of this plan) and returned ReadWrite, contradicting this repo's
        own Contact.object-meta.xml, which still declares ControlledByParent. The
        Record_Type_Matches_Account validation rule (Task 8) is what keeps a Contact's own
        record type from disagreeing with its parent Account's record type - without it a
        Broker Contact could be filed under an Investing_Entity Account (or vice versa) and the
        two segregation boundaries would silently diverge.

        ⚠ FIX 2026-08-10 (code review, CRITICAL) - EVERY PICKLIST ON THE OBJECT IS ENUMERATED
        BELOW - Salutation, LeadSource, OtherGeocodeAccuracy, MailingGeocodeAccuracy,
        ContactSource, TitleType, DepartmentGroup, BuyerAttributes, Broker_Priority__c,
        Broker_Specialty__c and Broker_Status__c - because a record type file that omits a
        picklist silently drops ALL of that picklist's values from that type. This is the same
        rule already enforced under NDA__c, Contract_Review__c, Disposition__c and LOI__c; the
        original brief that produced this file omitted it, which was a plan defect, not a
        deliberate narrowing. The full value set was pulled from a LIVE ORG DESCRIBE
        (`sf sobject describe, sobject=Contact`), never from this repo's field files, because
        `sf project retrieve` unions local and remote picklist values and a repo field file can
        list values the org no longer has. Both Contact record types (this one and Investor)
        carry the IDENTICAL, COMPLETE value set with the SAME defaults (Broker_Priority__c =
        Standard, Broker_Specialty__c = Retail, Broker_Status__c = Active) - narrowing either
        record type's values is a separate, deliberate change for later, and this fix is
        deliberately behaviour-neutral for every existing Contact.

        ⚠ FIX ROUND 2 (2026-08-10, check-only dry-run against usman-dpeg, deploy id
        0Afiw000000HR8fCAG) - THE ContactSource BLOCK NAMED ABOVE WAS REMOVED. The describe used
        to build the enumeration above reported ContactSource as a selectable picklist field
        (type=picklist, restrictedPicklist=true, 4 active values), but the Metadata API rejected
        it inside RecordType.picklistValues with "Field 'ContactSource' in 'Contact' is not a
        picklist field" - the describe and the Metadata API DISAGREED on this one field.

        A SECOND round of the same dry-run then rejected DepartmentGroup too, identical error
        shape: "Field 'DepartmentGroup' in 'Contact' is not a picklist field" - also removed.

        ⚠ FIX ROUND 3 (2026-08-10, iterative check-only dry-runs against usman-dpeg, root-caused
        by bisection) - Salutation, MailingGeocodeAccuracy AND OtherGeocodeAccuracy REMOVED. All
        three failed with a GENERIC "An unexpected error occurred... ErrorId..." rather than a
        clean field-level message, which made them far harder to diagnose than ContactSource/
        DepartmentGroup. Isolated by deploying single-picklist RecordType variants one at a time
        against usman-dpeg via the `metadata` flag targeting RecordType:Contact.Broker: LeadSource
        and the three custom fields deployed clean in isolation, both before and after the
        failing ones were tested, ruling out org-side state pollution as the explanation. THE
        ACTUAL ROOT CAUSE: all three failing fields are COMPOUND-FIELD SUB-COMPONENTS -
        `compoundFieldName` in the live describe reads `Name` for Salutation, `MailingAddress`
        for MailingGeocodeAccuracy, `OtherAddress` for OtherGeocodeAccuracy - confirmed by
        cross-checking `compoundFieldName` for every picklist on this object: every field that
        failed has one set, every field that passed does not. RecordType.picklistValues cannot
        restrict a compound field's sub-picklist; the platform does not report this as a clean
        validation error, it throws generically instead. This generalizes beyond Contact:
        Lead.Salutation/GeocodeAccuracy (`Name`/`Address`) and
        Account.BillingGeocodeAccuracy/ShippingGeocodeAccuracy (`BillingAddress`/
        `ShippingAddress`) were removed from their record types for the identical, confirmed
        reason - see those files' own comments. THE RULE: a picklist that is a compound-field
        sub-component must NEVER be enumerated in a RecordType's picklistValues - check
        `compoundFieldName` in the describe (not just `type`) before adding any field to this
        file. ⚠ TitleType was NOT individually isolation-tested in this round (its
        compoundFieldName is blank, so the compound-field theory correctly predicted it would be
        safe from THAT failure mode) - that gap is what Fix Round 4 closes.

        ⚠ FIX ROUND 4 (2026-08-10, full check-only dry-run against usman-dpeg after Round 3
        landed) - TitleType REMOVED. It failed with the SAME clean, specific error shape as
        ContactSource and DepartmentGroup: "Field 'TitleType' in 'Contact' is not a picklist
        field" - NOT the generic compound-field error. This is a THIRD field in this same
        describe/Metadata-API disagreement class, and it was missed in Round 2 (which only
        caught ContactSource and DepartmentGroup) because Round 2's dry-runs never got past the
        Lead businessProcess and compound-field errors far enough to reach TitleType's own
        validation. Notably, BuyerAttributes - a multipicklist from the SAME Salesforce
        "Contact enrichment" field family as TitleType/DepartmentGroup/ContactSource - is NOT in
        this failing set; it deployed clean throughout. There is no reliable a-priori signal
        (type, family, restrictedPicklist, compoundFieldName) that predicts which enrichment
        fields the Metadata API will refuse inside a RecordType - each one had to be proven by an
        actual dry-run. The remaining FIVE picklists below (LeadSource, Broker_Priority__c,
        Broker_Specialty__c, Broker_Status__c, BuyerAttributes) are the fields that survived ALL
        FOUR fix rounds and cleared a full dry-run of this exact file.
    -->
